00:47:56.800 --> 00:47:59.880
into them. And it's just really, it's a

00:47:59.880 --> 00:48:03.600
cool time to be alive and it's a cool time to be working as a

00:48:03.600 --> 00:48:06.640
filmmaker because you get inspired by these people that do

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:09.360
these amazing things, you know? And tell us something about the

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:10.880
project. Man, this seems like a very

00:48:11.400 --> 00:48:14.480
exotic project, something never seen before in the sport.

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:16.920
So yeah, what was it? Which?

00:48:16.960 --> 00:48:19.560
The VR project. Yeah, yeah.

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:22.680
Yeah. I mean, I think that project was

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:27.280
just really groundbreaking in so many ways, from speaking to John

00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:29.320
quite extensively about it at the time.

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:33.520
Who's John? John is the director behind it,

00:48:33.520 --> 00:48:37.520
John Griffith. He's the guy who, you know, he,

00:48:37.960 --> 00:48:39.840
he brought that whole production to life.

00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:45.480
He is kind of the guy in 360 VR Adventure sports who his first

00:48:45.480 --> 00:48:51.360
film was about going up Everest and then he did like a free solo

00:48:51.360 --> 00:48:54.680
film with Alex Honnold really, you know, like leading this

00:48:54.680 --> 00:49:01.080
charge in 360 virtual reality. He really, I would say there's

00:49:01.080 --> 00:49:04.240
probably no one else really. I mean, sorry if I'm cutting

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:06.920
people out there, but there's, you know, there's not many

00:49:06.920 --> 00:49:09.640
people working in that space. And I think that's for several

00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:14.160
reasons that that project was just really groundbreaking

00:49:14.160 --> 00:49:18.240
because before then he had only ever worked with cameras that

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:21.440
were held statically. So as a viewer, when you put on

00:49:21.440 --> 00:49:23.920
the headset, you weren't moving around at all.

00:49:23.920 --> 00:49:27.080
You know, you have basically mounted to some form of tripod

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:29.640
or pole or something like that. And then you were just sat there

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:31.640
kind of to observe on the action.

00:49:33.440 --> 00:49:37.160
And he had said, you know, to film flying that wouldn't work

00:49:37.160 --> 00:49:40.560
because you can't film flying statically flying.

00:49:40.680 --> 00:49:42.720
The whole point of flying is that you're moving in this

00:49:42.720 --> 00:49:45.560
three-dimensional. But the problem that they had

00:49:45.560 --> 00:49:49.640
always had in this 360 VR stuff is that when you put on a

00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:54.680
headset and you start to move, that you're actually, you know,

00:49:54.680 --> 00:49:58.400
realize you're stationary, your body doesn't like it and it

00:49:58.400 --> 00:50:00.760
makes you feel quite nauseous and sick.

00:50:00.840 --> 00:50:03.520
That can be really uncomfortable sensation.

00:50:04.040 --> 00:50:09.320
So John put in a huge amount of time and effort and investment

00:50:09.320 --> 00:50:12.880
in technology to work out how the camera could be moving but

00:50:12.880 --> 00:50:16.640
held very, what's the word on a gimbal or whatever.

00:50:16.640 --> 00:50:18.600
So there's no jerks, there's no wobbles.

00:50:18.600 --> 00:50:21.720
The horizon always stays the same and you don't suddenly

00:50:21.720 --> 00:50:25.120
speed up or you don't suddenly slow down because I think it's

00:50:25.120 --> 00:50:28.280
those forces of acceleration that you end up feeling sick.

00:50:29.200 --> 00:50:32.840
But to do that, it made the project really complicated in

00:50:32.840 --> 00:50:36.880
terms of how he needed to hold the camera when flying.

00:50:37.240 --> 00:50:42.880
So yeah, he they had the the tandem and below the tandem

00:50:43.360 --> 00:50:47.840
there was a gimbal which was attached to a big pole or maybe

00:50:47.840 --> 00:50:50.080
it was a pole and then the gimbal and then the camera don't

00:50:50.080 --> 00:50:52.200
remember exactly which way around with the technology

00:50:52.200 --> 00:50:56.480
worked. And then various kind of like

00:50:56.520 --> 00:50:59.880
fans and motors to hold everything so it didn't wobble.

00:51:02.160 --> 00:51:06.400
But you know, when you're taking off from places that are already

00:51:06.400 --> 00:51:09.640
quite an ambitious take off for a tandem once, ever taken off

00:51:09.640 --> 00:51:13.400
from there with a tandem before and then you have this hanging

00:51:13.400 --> 00:51:19.160
bag of stuff below you, It's pretty terrifying to watch and

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:22.160
potentially really expensive because you just have one small

00:51:22.160 --> 00:51:24.120
crash with that stuff and then it breaks.

00:51:24.120 --> 00:51:26.920
Then you know, they're all custom made stuff.

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:30.960
So I think the whole project was incredibly ambitious.

00:51:31.320 --> 00:51:34.440
And then where they pushed it, you know, we went and we landed

00:51:34.440 --> 00:51:37.600
up on sort of five and a half 1000 meters and camped there for

00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:41.120
the night with all those things following that story.

00:51:41.960 --> 00:51:46.160
It was just a very, very ambitious project that was

00:51:46.160 --> 00:51:48.600
already doing things with paragliders that's, you know,

00:51:48.600 --> 00:51:52.240
most people would regard as fairly cutting edge, and then

00:51:52.440 --> 00:51:55.040
having big drains along with you in this rig that I just

00:51:55.040 --> 00:51:58.160
described. So if you do have access to one

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:01.560
of these VR headsets, whether you own one or can go to a

00:52:01.560 --> 00:52:06.160
library or, or whatever it is, and you get to see that film, I

00:52:06.160 --> 00:52:13.360
think just bear in mind the sort of, yeah, the vision it took to

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:17.320
bring all that together. And actually there's, there is a

00:52:17.440 --> 00:52:21.000
behind the scenes film which I was involved in in, in filming

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:25.240
there, which doesn't show all of the things that that we did, but

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:28.840
definitely shows some of them. And I think you get a an idea of

00:52:29.200 --> 00:52:30.960
how much work that production took.

00:52:32.880 --> 00:52:36.160
OK, the thing that makes me so curious is that operating in

00:52:36.160 --> 00:52:39.240
these hostile environments, you know, I can label it like that

00:52:39.240 --> 00:52:43.560
because releasing the brake handles and bringing out your

00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:46.760
selfie stick itself is quite challenging a lot of times.

00:52:46.880 --> 00:52:55.880
But and having this whole rig and another person and close

00:52:55.880 --> 00:52:57.160
listening to it and another glider.

00:52:57.720 --> 00:53:01.560
What was the biggest challenge that you guys had while on this

00:53:01.560 --> 00:53:03.360
project? You know, because anything from

00:53:03.360 --> 00:53:06.280
sleeping overnight and freezing temperatures to waking up with a

00:53:06.840 --> 00:53:09.160
with a colleague with a bad mood, like, what was the time

00:53:09.160 --> 00:53:11.320
when nerves were running high on everyone?

00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:15.040
Yeah, I mean, maybe we can go back to the magic of freedom

00:53:15.040 --> 00:53:18.040
film because I feel like I can talk about that bit more freely

00:53:18.040 --> 00:53:25.320
because that was my production. So I think on any of these

00:53:26.080 --> 00:53:31.080
projects, you're, you know, you're kind of worried about

00:53:31.360 --> 00:53:34.800
your friends and yours safety in these in these places.

00:53:36.320 --> 00:53:39.240
And ultimately you are just out on an adventure.

00:53:39.520 --> 00:53:44.440
And in the Magic of Freedom, you know, Antoine Gerard, he's one

00:53:44.440 --> 00:53:47.160
of the sort of the main protagonists of that film.

00:53:48.160 --> 00:53:52.480
He decided he was going to try to climb this mountain called

00:53:52.480 --> 00:53:55.800
Duran, which is a little bit over 7000 meters.

00:53:56.760 --> 00:54:00.120
And he was going to do it by himself.

00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:04.200
How he came to doing it by himself was I think other people

00:54:04.200 --> 00:54:08.000
had been keen, but conditions and and sort of schedules and

00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:11.640
stuff of that trip just ended up being that he he did go for it

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:22.200
by himself and it was ended up being I think a pretty harrowing

00:54:22.200 --> 00:54:23.800
experience for Antoine in the end.

00:54:23.800 --> 00:54:27.560
You know, he made the summit, but he was moving through

00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:32.360
terrain. That is not the safest kind of

00:54:32.360 --> 00:54:36.080
terrain to be moving, even as a roped party because of crevasses

00:54:36.080 --> 00:54:38.880
and stuff like that. But at least if you are a party

00:54:38.880 --> 00:54:41.440
of two or three people, you can be tied together.

00:54:41.440 --> 00:54:45.680
And there is techniques for what happens if you fall in a in, in

00:54:45.680 --> 00:54:48.440
into a crevasse because you have a rope on and the other people

00:54:48.440 --> 00:54:50.680
can kind of act as like an anchor and then pull you out.

00:54:51.080 --> 00:54:56.280
But because he was by himself, yeah, there was quite a real

00:54:56.280 --> 00:55:01.440
chance that he could have fallen into a crevasse and gone a long

00:55:01.440 --> 00:55:04.800
way into it and either died from falling because some of them

00:55:04.800 --> 00:55:09.360
are, you know, like you're 70 meters deep or just fallen down

00:55:09.640 --> 00:55:13.320
and not, you know, being able to get himself out because he's

00:55:13.320 --> 00:55:15.600
fallen 10 meters. And maybe he wouldn't have been

00:55:15.600 --> 00:55:17.880
injured, but he couldn't climb out for whatever reason.

00:55:18.560 --> 00:55:23.560
And yeah, so that really was taking a lot of kind of risks by

00:55:23.560 --> 00:55:28.960
doing that summit by himself. And we lost comes with Antoine.

00:55:29.600 --> 00:55:32.160
You know, we had been in comes with him on the way up, but then

00:55:32.160 --> 00:55:35.600
we kind of lost comes with him. And it was pretty worrying, you

00:55:35.600 --> 00:55:37.960
know, the next morning, we still weren't able to get him.

00:55:38.240 --> 00:55:41.160
And as you see in the film, the weather sort of starts to

00:55:41.160 --> 00:55:44.280
change. And there was, you know, a real

00:55:44.280 --> 00:55:47.440
genuine nervousness for him amongst us.

00:55:47.800 --> 00:55:50.400
And you start worrying about like, well, how are we going to

00:55:50.400 --> 00:55:54.280
launch any kind of practical rescue mission with the weather

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:58.360
coming in? And in the end, he was able to

00:55:58.360 --> 00:56:00.880
take off. But I think it was kind of a

00:56:00.880 --> 00:56:03.520
pretty close thing. And he was suffering from

00:56:03.520 --> 00:56:06.760
altitude sickness and hadn't had any real sleep the night before

00:56:06.760 --> 00:56:11.320
and stuff. And he got down just in time,

00:56:12.600 --> 00:56:15.840
which, you know, was amazing. And you know, he did manage to

00:56:15.840 --> 00:56:18.640
get himself off and he did manage with with the skill and

00:56:19.120 --> 00:56:21.600
stuff that he had said to pull it all off.

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:24.920
But it was a close run thing because you get these huge cloud

00:56:24.920 --> 00:56:27.800
bursts come through with like large amounts of wind and stuff.

00:56:27.800 --> 00:56:31.240
And that that did come in, you know, in the film, you see it

00:56:31.240 --> 00:56:33.400
happen, feels like it happens pretty quick.

00:56:33.400 --> 00:56:36.720
And that was the reality. You know, we were then kind of

00:56:36.720 --> 00:56:40.160
like an hour of him landing. We had this huge weather storm

00:56:40.160 --> 00:56:42.720
come in. And we're talking gusts of wind

00:56:42.720 --> 00:56:45.680
when they happen in in in that place of Koruna bad.

00:56:46.320 --> 00:56:49.600
I I wouldn't be able to give you an actual gauge.

00:56:49.600 --> 00:56:52.160
Aaron, what they are. Aaron described that even the

00:56:52.160 --> 00:56:53.880
stones on the road were rolling. It was.

00:56:53.960 --> 00:56:56.040
Yeah, exactly. I mean, that is what happens and

00:56:56.040 --> 00:56:58.040
you see it several times in a season.

00:56:58.120 --> 00:57:01.200
And when it happens, you you could look at the sky.

00:57:01.200 --> 00:57:03.560
If you arrived at the place you'd be like, oh, we could do

00:57:03.560 --> 00:57:05.520
fly now. It just looks a bit overcast,

00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:09.080
but it's actually a, you know, one or two, several big storm

00:57:09.080 --> 00:57:12.640
cells above you. And yeah, like, there's dust

00:57:12.640 --> 00:57:14.800
blowing everywhere. Birds are getting like, pushed

00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:17.280
out of the sky, plastic bags getting blown everywhere.

00:57:17.280 --> 00:57:20.160
It's if you are under a paraglider in those moments,

00:57:20.160 --> 00:57:23.680
it's it's really serious. So yeah, I guess what I'm trying

00:57:23.680 --> 00:57:27.160
to say is that when we are in these places that the adventure

00:57:27.160 --> 00:57:33.200
kind of comes first and the filming second because it's it's

00:57:33.200 --> 00:57:35.320
a proper adventure. And you know you're doing your

00:57:35.320 --> 00:57:38.560
best to capture what you can, but you also have to look after

00:57:38.560 --> 00:57:40.040
yourself and look after your friends.

00:57:40.960 --> 00:57:44.760
I I've seen that and I I can completely understand what you

00:57:44.760 --> 00:57:48.480
mean because I think Antoine did not even speak much when he

00:57:48.480 --> 00:57:52.480
landed to capture his expressions or emotions because

00:57:52.480 --> 00:57:54.760
he was just like, yeah, you know, wanted to finish it.

00:57:54.760 --> 00:57:57.880
And you could feel the distress in her in his voice when he was

00:57:57.880 --> 00:58:01.200
recording that difficult night on the round.

00:58:01.520 --> 00:58:03.960
And I don't think he recorded the take off.

00:58:03.960 --> 00:58:06.520
He just recorded mid flight for the sake of it.

00:58:06.880 --> 00:58:11.240
But you know, the question that I want to ask you is, and this

00:58:11.240 --> 00:58:13.520
can probably be a message to the viewers as well.

00:58:14.080 --> 00:58:17.800
A lot of times whenever we want to portray something that we do,

00:58:18.200 --> 00:58:21.320
we hide our deepest vulnerabilities.

00:58:21.320 --> 00:58:25.320
It's, it's not comfortable bringing out our things that

00:58:25.320 --> 00:58:30.960
scare us, especially in this context when things can go

00:58:31.800 --> 00:58:37.080
haywire pretty quickly and, and emotions can go on a sinusoidal,

00:58:37.080 --> 00:58:39.800
you know, like one time you're super high, one time you're

00:58:39.800 --> 00:58:45.960
super low. We only tend to share one side

00:58:45.960 --> 00:58:50.560
of the emotion. I somehow feel that sharing

00:58:50.560 --> 00:58:52.960
reality is more important than sharing just the good side.

00:58:55.320 --> 00:58:59.080
Don't want to put you in a spot by any means but Red Bull X Alps

00:58:59.320 --> 00:59:04.120
constantly censors their output that has been given to their

00:59:04.120 --> 00:59:08.080
audiences. Umm don't.

00:59:08.160 --> 00:59:11.320
What's your thought on this man? Like we as pilots should be

00:59:11.320 --> 00:59:14.760
sharing the reality right? Like, OK, you get to fly over

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:18.640
the clouds, but hey, this is the price that might come with it,

00:59:19.280 --> 00:59:23.240
so be aware. Yeah, I mean, I can't speak for

00:59:23.240 --> 00:59:27.240
Red Bull X Alps, and I have no idea what they censor or don't

00:59:27.240 --> 00:59:30.000
censor, so I can't really comment on that.

00:59:30.760 --> 00:59:33.920
I think I guess it goes with human nature.

00:59:33.920 --> 00:59:38.200
I mean, if you look at people's social media feeds or whatever,

00:59:38.200 --> 00:59:41.760
they're often filled with highlights of life and the best

00:59:41.760 --> 00:59:45.720
moments. But that's not to say that I

00:59:45.720 --> 00:59:48.840
feel like actually what social media actually often does is

00:59:49.040 --> 00:59:52.040
filters out in the middle. Because of course, when someone,

00:59:52.520 --> 00:59:55.480
when people do hurt themselves, they often do share those

00:59:55.480 --> 00:59:58.600
stories. OK, some people don't want to

00:59:58.600 --> 01:00:01.520
share an accident, and I think that's totally fine.

01:00:01.520 --> 01:00:05.200
Like it's a very personal decision as to what you want to

01:00:05.200 --> 01:00:09.040
share. But when we scroll through a lot

01:00:09.040 --> 01:00:13.520
of social media is often the highlights of our lives.

01:00:14.680 --> 01:00:16.480
And I guess that's kind of natural.

01:00:16.480 --> 01:00:21.720
But I think there's of course value in, in sharing, you know,

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:26.840
whatever you want to, but from a storytelling point of view.

01:00:27.600 --> 01:00:31.520
You know, if everything goes perfectly, often it actually

01:00:31.520 --> 01:00:36.440
doesn't make for a very interesting story from point of

01:00:36.440 --> 01:00:39.520
view of someone who is interested in making documentary

01:00:39.520 --> 01:00:42.400
films and stuff. You're often not looking for

01:00:42.400 --> 01:00:46.200
perfect moments. You want to capture when things

01:00:46.200 --> 01:00:49.760
don't maybe go as planned, because those are the

01:00:49.760 --> 01:00:53.720
interesting parts. And I think in The Magic of

01:00:53.720 --> 01:01:00.080
Freedom, Antoine did give a very open and honest expression of

01:01:00.080 --> 01:01:02.320
what happened when he had just landed there.

01:01:02.320 --> 01:01:05.040
He was quite obviously, like, hard.

01:01:05.040 --> 01:01:07.040
And you could see the fear in him.

01:01:07.040 --> 01:01:11.440
And it was a really raw moment. And we had been super worried

01:01:11.440 --> 01:01:13.560
about him. And, you know, you can see how

01:01:13.560 --> 01:01:17.440
tired he is and stuff. And yeah, I guess from a film

01:01:17.440 --> 01:01:21.640
point of view, if we only capture the peak moments, then

01:01:21.640 --> 01:01:23.880
there's nothing to balance that out and there's nothing to make

01:01:23.880 --> 01:01:27.680
it show why that thing is is hard and worthy of people's

01:01:27.680 --> 01:01:31.960
attention. I think from people wanting to

01:01:31.960 --> 01:01:35.520
make their own content, like you said, I'm not sure if it

01:01:35.520 --> 01:01:39.240
necessarily has to be, you know, these like low moments or middle

01:01:39.240 --> 01:01:42.360
moments or whatever. But I think if you want to make

01:01:42.360 --> 01:01:45.800
something that people are going to connect with, there needs to

01:01:45.800 --> 01:01:50.840
be that human element. Yeah, it's that that human

01:01:50.840 --> 01:01:56.440
element is what makes US feel things and want to connect with

01:01:56.440 --> 01:02:00.480
people. And good storytelling usually

01:02:00.480 --> 01:02:02.800
has that. Not always, but often.

01:02:03.280 --> 01:02:05.760
And I mean, there's so many different ways.

01:02:05.760 --> 01:02:07.960
And reflecting back on what you said with the Everest thing,

01:02:07.960 --> 01:02:10.600
which is just raw and cut, you know, that's very powerful in

01:02:10.600 --> 01:02:12.880
itself. But most of the time, if you

01:02:12.880 --> 01:02:17.440
just roll with a GoPro, filming it can be really beautiful, but

01:02:17.440 --> 01:02:20.480
you don't necessarily actually connect to that experience as

01:02:20.480 --> 01:02:24.680
much as when someone's telling you about it or see the joy or

01:02:24.680 --> 01:02:27.880
the the fear or whatever it might be on on their face in

01:02:27.880 --> 01:02:31.960
those moments. That is so well said my man.

01:02:32.720 --> 01:02:35.600
I am totally satisfied with your answer and I hope the viewers

01:02:35.640 --> 01:02:38.240
out as well. I'm glad glad it helps.

